-- Schema v2 → v3: sweep the rows orphaned while foreign keys were off.
--
-- Every child table in this schema declares ON DELETE CASCADE, and none
-- of it has ever fired. SQLite leaves foreign-key enforcement off unless
-- something turns it on, nothing did, and `AndroidSqliteDriver` does not
-- do it for you — so `UserRepository.deleteProfile` has been leaving the
-- deleted profile's cubes, solves and settings behind. Invisible, but
-- present, and referenced by nothing.
--
-- `DriverFactory` now enables the pragma. That fixes every future
-- deletion and nothing at all about the past: enforcement validates what
-- you write, not what is already stored, so without this sweep the old
-- rows would sit there for the life of the database — counted by
-- `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` and by nothing else.
--
-- `cube_names` cannot actually hold orphans: `1.sqm` filters them out on
-- the way in, and every write since goes through an enforced foreign key.
-- It is swept anyway. This file is the one place that answers "what
-- happened to what the missing cascades left behind", and a sweep that
-- names four tables and finds nothing in one of them reads better than
-- three tables and a paragraph explaining the fourth.
DELETE FROM settings   WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM users);
DELETE FROM solves     WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM users);
DELETE FROM cubes      WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM users);
DELETE FROM cube_names WHERE user_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM users);
